\begin_layout Section
Qualitative Observations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
When is N 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

large enough
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
For me, generally speaking, if a number is larger than the number of my
 fingers (
\begin_inset Formula $\approx10$
\end_inset

), I consider it to be infinity.
 For some people, nothing is ever large enough...
 In our context, the question boils down to how well the discrete sum 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum J_{ij}r_{j}$
\end_inset

 approximates the integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\tilde{J}(\theta-\varphi)r(\varphi,t)d\varphi$
\end_inset

.
 This is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $dt$
\end_inset

 and maybe more (like the method of numerical solver).
 Basically, as N is larger, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\theta=\frac{2\pi}{N}$
\end_inset

 is smaller which makes the discrete sum closer to the integral.
 From other simulations I ran, I found that in my case when 
\begin_inset Formula $N=3$
\end_inset

 the simulation does not diverge for 
\begin_inset Formula $J=2.1$
\end_inset

, however, for 
\begin_inset Formula $5\le N$
\end_inset

, it does (as could be seen in the graphs below).
 Generally, I would choose 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\theta$
\end_inset

 is sensitive enough to changes in the different regimes of the ring.
 Which in our case is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\theta<\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Even better is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\theta\ll1$
\end_inset

.
 All in all, we can see the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $N=1001$
\end_inset

, in this particular case, is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good enough
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

